## Keeping an older draft

You have rewritten a chapter and you want the old one where you can still read it. Not in the book, not in the way, but not gone either.

Put it in another binder.

Duplicate the chapter, rename the copy so you will know it later, and drag it into *Research* or *Notes*, or into a binder of your own. That is all. It stays a real chapter: you can open it, read it beside the new one, search it, and move it back if the rewrite turns out to be the worse of the two.

## Why another binder is enough

A book runs from its own marker to the next one, and it never runs past the edge of the binder it lives in. So the moment your old chapter is in another binder it is outside the book, and every figure that measures the book stops counting it: the word count, your targets and pace, the export, and the analyses that read the book against itself.

That last one is the reason this matters more than it looks. Repetition, echoes, near-duplicate scenes and vocabulary variety all work by comparing your manuscript with itself. An old draft is not merely extra text to them, it is *the same text twice*, which is the one thing that makes all of those readings wrong at once. Every echo doubles. Every scene has a near-twin. Move the copy out of the manuscript binder and they are right again.

## What still works on it

Everything about reading and editing. The old chapter keeps its title and its place in whatever binder you put it in, and it is still found by search, still spellchecked, still backed up, still in your version history.

Your story bible still works there too. If a character is named in the old draft, that page still counts it as a place she appears, because knowing where you have written about someone is a different question from what your book is made of.

## The other ways, and when they fit

*Trash* is for something you have decided against. A trashed row leaves every count and every list, and stays recoverable, but it is not a place to read from.

*Version history* keeps what each piece of text said before, and can put an older state back. It is the right tool for "what did this paragraph say yesterday". It is not the right tool for this, because it works one row at a time and restoring replaces what is there now: it cannot hold two drafts open side by side, and you cannot give an old state a name or a place in the binder.

*Turning off "Include in exports"* keeps a row out of the exported file, and that is all it was ever asked to do. It is what you want for something that belongs to the project but not the printed book. It does not take a row out of the manuscript, so an old draft left in place would still be measured against the book it is a copy of.
